<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 — Not All Tokens Are Equal -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Not All Tokens Are Equal</h2>
<p>
  Standard Mixture-of-Experts routing applies a fixed top-<em>k</em> selection: every token, without exception, activates exactly K expert FFN networks. The word "the" gets the same number of expert computations as "eigenvector". This is a fundamental mismatch between computational budget and token complexity.
</p>
<p>
  For easy tokens — function words, punctuation, determiners — activating eight full FFN networks is pure waste. For hard tokens — rare technical terms, multi-step reasoning pivots, ambiguous contexts — eight experts may not be enough. A static top-k treats a comma the same as a complex mathematical symbol.
</p>
<p>
  LongCat-Flash's answer is to let the model learn which tokens need more compute and which need less, while keeping the average constant. The visualization below shows the contrast: fixed routing gives both simple and complex tokens identical compute budgets, while the ZCE approach distributes compute where it matters.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">routing-comparison.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Fixed vs dynamic expert allocation</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.875rem">
    <canvas id="routing-canvas" width="680" height="240"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">Key Insight</div>
  In a 512-expert MoE with top-8 routing, approximately 98.4% of expert capacity sits idle for every single token — yet the model cannot route more capacity to the tokens that genuinely need it. ZCE breaks this constraint by making "do nothing" a valid expert choice.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 — Zero-Computation Experts -->
<section id="s-zce" data-code="zce">
<div class="section-no">02 — Zero-Computation Experts</div>
<h2>Zero-Computation Experts</h2>
<p>
  LongCat-Flash augments the standard MoE pool with 256 <strong>Zero-Computation Experts (ZCE)</strong>. Unlike FFN experts that transform the input through a learned two-layer network, a ZCE simply returns its input unchanged: <em>E&#x2ᵢ(x) = x</em>. Routing to a ZCE costs no FLOPs — it is a mathematical identity.
</p>
<p>
  The combined expert pool has N = 512 FFN experts plus Z = 256 identity experts, for 768 total. The router selects K = 12 experts per token from this pool. A token that routes to 9 identity experts activates only 3 FFN experts; a token that routes to 1 identity expert activates 11 FFN experts. The model learns this routing end-to-end.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">ZCE Forward Pass</div>
  <span class="math-var">MoE(x<sub>t</sub>)</span> = &Sigma;<sub>i &isin; top-K</sub> g<sub>i</sub> &middot; E<sub>i</sub>(x<sub>t</sub>)<br>
  where <span class="math-var">E<sub>i</sub>(x) = FFN<sub>i</sub>(x)</span> for i &le; N &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// real FFN expert</span><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-var">E<sub>i</sub>(x) = x</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;for i &gt; N &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// identity (zero compute)</span>
</div>

<p>
  A <strong>PID controller</strong> adjusts routing biases so that, averaged across all tokens in a batch, exactly ~8 FFN experts fire per token. But the per-token count has a measured standard deviation of 3 — meaning at any given token the model may use 5 FFN experts or 11. Click each token below to see how compute is distributed:
</p>

<div class="vp">
