        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tok-stat">
      Active params this token: <span id="tok-params">~18.6B</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp;
      Compute vs max: <span id="tok-compute">25%</span> &nbsp;|&nbsp;
      Classification: <span id="tok-class">easy (function word)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The key benefit: the global average remains at 8 FFN experts (27B active parameters), satisfying the overall efficiency target. But the model gains the freedom to express that "the" is trivial and "backpropagation" deserves extra capacity. Training converges to a standard deviation of 3 activated FFN experts, confirming that the model learns to use this freedom.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 — ScMoE -->
<section id="s-scmoe" data-code="scmoe">
<div class="section-no">03 — Hiding Communication in Plain Sight</div>
<h2>Shortcut-Connected MoE</h2>
<p>
  In any distributed MoE system, tokens must be dispatched to the GPUs hosting their selected experts before FFN computation can begin. This dispatch-then-compute sequence creates mandatory idle bubbles: the GPU sits waiting for all-to-all communication to finish before it can do any useful work.
</p>
<p>
  For LongCat-Flash running on 128 H800 GPUs, the non-overlapping communication time was measured at <strong>25.3%</strong> of total step time — a massive inefficiency. DeepSeek-V3's Two Batch Overlap (TBO) technique reduced this to ~16% by overlapping two micro-batches, but the fundamental issue remained.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Shortcut-Connected MoE (ScMoE)</strong> takes a different approach. It adds a cross-layer shortcut so the dense FFN computation from layer <em>N-1</em> runs in parallel with the dispatch of layer <em>N</em>. The overlap window expands from a single batch to two adjacent layers, dropping non-overlapping communication to just <strong>8.4%</strong>.
</p>

