<p>
  For inference, ScMoE enables the <strong>Single Batch Overlap (SBO)</strong> pipeline, which achieves a theoretical TPOT (time per output token) of 16ms — nearly half the 30ms of DeepSeek-V3's Two Batch Overlap approach. The SBO pipeline has four stages: (1) Multi-head Latent Attention alone (cannot be overlapped), (2) Dispatch overlapping with Dense FFN and QKV projection, (3) MoE GEMM independently, and (4) Combine overlapping with Core Attention and Dense FFN.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Non-overlapping communication: 25.3% (standard) → 8.4% (ScMoE)</li>
  <li>Theoretical TPOT: 30ms (TBO) → 16ms (SBO)</li>
  <li>Output token cost: $0.17/M (DSV3) → $0.09/M (LongCat-Flash)</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Scaling Without Crashing -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">04 — Scaling Without Crashing</div>
<h2>Scaling Without Crashing</h2>
<p>
  Training a 560B parameter MoE model from scratch introduces severe instability risks: gradient explosions, router collapse, BF16 overflow from massive activations, and hyperparameters that cannot be swept at full scale. LongCat-Flash addresses each with a four-technique stability framework.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">stability-framework.navigator</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Step through the four techniques</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="step-list">
      <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Model Growth — Layer Stacking</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Train a 14-layer half-scale model first (~10B tokens), then stack two copies to initialize the 28-layer target. The stacked model's loss initially rises, then converges faster than a randomly initialized 28-layer model — better initialization at lower cost than training the full model from scratch.</div>
          <div class="step-code">small = train(layers=14) → stack(small, small) → large</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Hyperparameter Transfer — Width Scaling</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Use a proxy model at width 768 (scaling factor s = 6144/768 = 8) to sweep learning rate &#951; and initialization variance &#963;². Transfer to the 560B target via &#951;_target = &#951;_proxy / s and &#963;²_target = &#963;²_proxy / s. This eliminates expensive sweeps on the full model.</div>
          <div class="step-code">&#951;_target = &#951;_proxy / 8 &nbsp;&nbsp; &#963;²_target = &#963;²_proxy / 8</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Router Stability — Gradient Norm Ratio</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Monitor the gradient norm ratio R<sub>g</sub> = ||&#945;&#8711;L<sub>LB</sub>|| / ||&#8711;L<sub>LM</sub>||, where L<sub>LB</sub> is the load-balancing loss. Keep R<sub>g</sub> &lt; 0.1 so the balancing signal acts as a regularizer without dominating routing specialization. If R<sub>g</sub> rises, reduce the balance coefficient &#945;.</div>
          <div class="step-code">R&#x261;  = ||&#945; &#8711;L_LB|| / ||&#8711;L_LM|| &lt; 0.1</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Hidden z-Loss + Adam &#949; = 1e-16</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Massive hidden activations (&gt;10&#8308; magnitude) cause BF16 overflow. A tiny hidden z-loss term penalizes log-sum-exp of activation magnitudes — it kills spikes without noticeably affecting language modeling loss. Adam &#949; is set to 1e-16 (vs default 1e-5) because at large scale, gradient RMS values are tiny; default &#949; dominates the denominator and destroys the adaptive property.</div>
          <div class="step-code">&#949; = 1e-16 &nbsp;(vs default 1e-5) &nbsp;&nbsp; Adam &#949; &lt; grad_RMS</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>&#8592; Prev</button>
      <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">1 / 4</span>
      <button id="step-next">Next &#8594;</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Together these four techniques allowed the team to train 20T tokens in 30 days on 6,144 H800 GPUs with 98.48% uptime — approximately 1.5% of compute lost to hardware failures and restarts, a remarkable result for a training run of this scale.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 — Building Agentic Intelligence -->
<section id="s-agentic" data-code="agentic">
<div class="section-no">05 — Building Agentic Intelligence</div>
<h2>Building Agentic Intelligence</h2>
<p>
  Post-training for agentic tasks presents a data scarcity problem. Real agentic trajectories — where a model calls tools, waits for results, and iterates — are expensive to collect and rarely cover the diversity of real-world tool ecosystems. LongCat-Flash addresses this with a <strong>multi-agent synthesis framework</strong> that generates diverse, verifiable, difficulty-calibrated training tasks.
</p>
<p>
  Task difficulty is characterized on three independent axes: <em>information processing complexity</em> (length and depth of reasoning chains), <em>tool-set complexity</em> (density and branching of the tool dependency graph), and <em>user interaction complexity</em> (how many clarifying exchanges are needed before the goal is clear). The framework builds 80,000 mock tools across 1,600 apps in 40 domains.
</p>

